  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: We are still expecting progressively milder days, \ncool nights, and little to no precipitation through at least \nThursday.\n\nTwo main features will keep us on the dry side through the middle \npart of next week. First, a broad, deep area of low pressure will \nmove north along the coast of the mid-Atlatnic region. Precipitation \nassociated with this feature is expected to remain primarily east \nand northeast of the region. Timing and location has fluctuated as to \nwhen eastern portions of the area may be glanced by associated \nshowers. What had been a Sunday/Monday potential, has transitioned \nto a Saturday night potential. Second, as this deep low makes \nslow progression north towards and into New England, an upper \nlevel ridge and surface high pressure are expected to build over\nour region by the early portion of next week. This will \ncontinue the trend of dry weather, but with building heights \nwill come temperatures which will trend milder. Temperatures \nwill gradually trend from values averaging around or a few \ndegrees below normal this weekend to values a few degrees above \nnormal by Thursday.",
